Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 05:01:50 +0000
Subject: [RFC PATCH 0/2] hid: ipts: fix a list race and bound the stop path (Surface Pro 7)

These two patches fix two touchscreen driver (ipts) faults seen on a
Surface Pro 7 (EDS2, poll mode). They apply to drivers/hid/ipts of
linux-surface/kernel v7.2-surface-devel (2dfd333c3af7) and, unchanged,
v7.3-surface-devel (87cf7d2e0ca7); drivers/hid/ipts is the same on both.

Status: RFC. Patch 1 is the same fix as linux-surface/kernel PR #178, so
it is here for reference only; the plan is to support #178 there rather
than open a duplicate. Patch 2 is not ready to merge: a review found a
use-after-free on its "message lock lost" path (see Known issues), and
its rework is not done.

1/2 hid: ipts: find and unlink a reply under one lock
    ipts_mei_search() found a reply under the read lock and deleted it
    under a second, separately taken write lock. Two waiters could take
    the same entry, and the second list_del() faulted
    (0xdead000000000108, RIP ipts_mei_search).

2/2 hid: ipts: bound the stop path and never unload with a live HID
    device
    ipts_thread_stop() waited with no timeout, so after the oops above
    every later stop hung. Reboot, power-off and rmmod hangs were
    captured (reboot stack from pstore: device_shutdown >
    pci_device_shutdown > mei_me_shutdown > mei_stop > ... >
    ipts_remove > ... > ipts_thread_stop > wait_for_completion);
    suspend takes the same path and is expected to hang, but that was
    not observed. A failed stop handshake also skipped
    ipts_hid_free(), so the module could unload with its HID device
    still registered.

What was tested (Surface Pro 7, kernel 6.19.8, code identical to these
two patches plus a test-only fault-injection patch that is compiled out
by default; evidence files named in brackets):
- Before the fix: the stock driver faulted in the first start-up race
  cycle in 4 of 4 runs [R2/red-summary.txt], and a plain reboot after
  that fault hung in ipts_thread_stop() [R1/verdict.txt].
- Start-up race (ipts-race.sh, 4 processes sending HID requests while
  the driver loads): 200/200 cycles, 0 kernel errors
  [R2/green-summary.txt].
- Unload with a failed stop handshake (fault injection, 4 processes
  using the hidraw node): 200 cycles, HID device gone after every
  unload, 0 kernel errors; 2 runs ended in a firmware stall (the device
  stops answering until a reboot) [R3/green-summary.txt].
- Receiver thread killed while holding message_lock, and a userspace
  request keeping it: sleep completes, reboot back in 32-40 s, 0 new
  kernel errors, 3 runs each [R4/thread-summary.txt,
  R4/caller-summary.txt].
- 50/50 suspend/resume cycles, one 61-minute sleep, 0 new kernel
  errors [R6/summary.txt]; 10/10 normal restarts back in 90 s or less
  [R7/restarts.txt].
- The bracketed evidence files are local test records, available on
  request.
- Patch 1 alone was also run as the installed driver from 2026-10-04;
  it builds to the same srcversion as that module (F510A3A9942A...).
- A 7.3.0-rc7 kernel built with these two patches (its in-tree ipts
  module reports srcversion D2A20EFC6712...; external-module builds of
  the same source report B1F111A44FE3...) has been the default kernel
  since 2026-10-19. On its
  first checked boot ipts loaded, iptsd was running and no Oops or BUG
  line was logged. None of the runs above were repeated on 7.3.
- Both patches were rebased onto v7.2-surface-devel with no conflicts;
  every prefix builds as an external module with 0 warnings, and
  checkpatch.pl --strict reports only the missing Signed-off-by.

What was not tested:
- EDS1 devices (no hardware). Patch 2 moves the EDS1 mode-switch restart
  into a work item; the EDS1 files themselves are unchanged.
- Any device other than one Surface Pro 7.
- A lock holder that stays alive and stuck (see Known issues).
- Patch 2 does not apply on top of #178 as is (conflict in mei.c). A
  version rebased onto #178, with the conflict resolved, gives a tree
  byte-identical to this series; it was built but not run.

Known issues:
- Patch 2, use-after-free: once message_lock is declared lost (three
  1 s timeouts with the same holder), ipts_control_stop() returns
  -EDEADLK and ipts_remove() frees the context. A holder that is stuck
  but still alive later calls up_write() on freed memory.
- Patch 2 pins the module with try_module_get() and loops forever in
  remove if the module is already going away; reviewers are likely to
  reject that, and the planned rework drops it.
- After heavy unload/reload churn the IPTS firmware can stop answering
  until the next reboot (about 1 in 110-140 reloads under stress). It
  was first seen without patch 2, on a driver with only patch 1 (cycle
  4 of an unload/open run). Before that, 100 cycles of the same race on
  that driver showed no stall (raw logs not kept), which is not enough
  data to call it a difference.

AI disclosure: an AI coding assistant (Claude Code, an LLM) did most of
the work: finding both bugs from crash records and the source, writing
both patches and these messages, and running the tests on the device.
Only the human submitter adds a Signed-off-by, after reviewing both
patches. Both patches carry "Assisted-by: LLM". No specialized analysis
tools (sparse, smatch, coccinelle) were used; checkpatch.pl was run.

